\begin{cases}
2 - \sqrt{x - 2} & \text{if } x > 2, \\
2 + (x-2)^2 & \text{if } x \leq 2.
\end{cases}
\] To verify, we can check that $f(f(x)) = x$ for both cases. For $x \leq 2$, $f(x) = 2 + (x-2)^2$. If $2 + (x-2)^2 \leq 2$, then $f(f(x)) = f(2 + (x-2)^2) = 2 - \sqrt{(2 + (x-2)^2) - 2} = 2 - \sqrt{(x-2)^2} = 2 - |x-2| = x$ (since $x \leq 2$, $|x-2| = 2 - x$). For $x > 2$, $f(x) = 2 - \sqrt{x - 2}$. If $2 - \sqrt{x - 2} \leq 2$, then $f(f(x)) = f(2 - \sqrt{x - 2}) = 2 + ((2 - \sqrt{x - 2}) - 2)^2 = 2 + (\sqrt{x - 2})^2 = 2 + (x - 2) = x$. Therefore, the function $f(x)$ is its own inverse. </think> <answer> $2 - \sqrt{x - 2}$ </answer>

